[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Enums: Definition, Usage, and Best Practices in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums, short for enumerations, are a special data type in Java that allows a variable to be one of a set of predefined constants. They provide a way to define a type that represents a fixed set of possible values. Enums enhance type safety, readability, and maintainability compared to using integer constants or String literals."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Definition of Enums"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An enum is declared using the `enum` keyword. Each enum constant is implicitly `public static final`. By convention, enum constants are written in uppercase."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"enum Day {
  SUNDAY, MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `Day` is an enum representing the days of the week. `SUNDAY`, `MONDAY`, etc., are the enum constants."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage of Enums"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums can be used like any other data type. You can declare variables of an enum type and assign enum constants to them."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class EnumExample {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Day today = Day.WEDNESDAY;

    if (today == Day.WEDNESDAY) {
      System.out.println("Today is Wednesday!");
    }

    System.out.println("Today is: " + today);
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code declares a variable `today` of type `Day` and assigns it the value `Day.WEDNESDAY`. It then checks if `today` is equal to `Day.WEDNESDAY` and prints a message. The `toString()` method is implicitly called when printing the enum constant."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Enums with Fields and Methods"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums can have fields and methods, just like classes. This allows you to associate data and behavior with each enum constant.  Constructors are implicitly private."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"enum Planet {
  MERCURY(3.303e+23, 2.4397e6),
  VENUS  (4.869e+24, 6.0518e6),
  EARTH  (5.976e+24, 6.37814e6),
  MARS   (6.421e+23, 3.3972e6),
  JUPITER(1.9e+27,   7.1492e7),
  SATURN (5.688e+26, 6.0268e7),
  URANUS (8.686e+25, 2.5559e7),
  NEPTUNE(1.024e+26, 2.4746e7);

  private final double mass;   // in kilograms
  private final double radius; // in meters
  Planet(double mass, double radius) {
    this.mass = mass;
    this.radius = radius;
  }
  public double mass()   { return mass; }
  public double radius() { return radius; }

  // universal gravitational constant  (m3 kg-1 s-2)
  public static final double G = 6.67300E-11;

  double surfaceGravity() {
    return G * mass / (radius * radius);
  }
  double surfaceWeight(double otherMass) {
    return otherMass * surfaceGravity();
  }
}

public class PlanetExample {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    double earthWeight = 75;
    double mass = earthWeight/Planet.EARTH.surfaceGravity();
    for (Planet p : Planet.values())
      System.out.printf("Your weight on %s is %f%n",
                        p, p.surfaceWeight(mass));
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `Planet` enum has fields for mass and radius, a constructor to initialize these fields, and methods to access them and calculate surface gravity and weight. The `values()` method, automatically generated for enums, returns an array of all the enum constants."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Interfaces"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums can implement interfaces, allowing you to define common behavior across different enum types. This is useful for creating polymorphic code."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"interface Operation {
  double apply(double x, double y);
}

enum BasicOperation implements Operation {
  PLUS("+") {
    public double apply(double x, double y) { return x + y; }
  },
  MINUS("-") {
    public double apply(double x, double y) { return x - y; }
  },
  TIMES("*") {
    public double apply(double x, double y) { return x * y; }
  },
  DIVIDE("/") {
    public double apply(double x, double y) { return x / y; }
  };

  private final String symbol;

  BasicOperation(String symbol) {
    this.symbol = symbol;
  }

  @Override
  public String toString() {
    return symbol;
  }
}

public class OperationExample {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    double x = 4;
    double y = 2;
    for (BasicOperation op : BasicOperation.values())
      System.out.printf("%f %s %f = %f%n",
              x, op, y, op.apply(x, y));
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `BasicOperation` enum implements the `Operation` interface, providing a specific implementation of the `apply` method for each enum constant.  Each constant is effectively an anonymous inner class."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"EnumSet and EnumMap"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"`EnumSet` and `EnumMap` are specialized collection classes designed for use with enums. `EnumSet` is a highly efficient set implementation for enum types, and `EnumMap` is a highly efficient map implementation for enum keys."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.EnumSet;

enum Color {
  RED, GREEN, BLUE
}

public class EnumSetExample {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    EnumSet<Color> colors = EnumSet.of(Color.RED, Color.GREEN);

    System.out.println("Colors: " + colors);

    EnumSet<Color> allColors = EnumSet.allOf(Color.class);
    System.out.println("All Colors: " + allColors);

    EnumSet<Color> emptySet = EnumSet.noneOf(Color.class);
    System.out.println("Empty Set: " + emptySet);
  }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.EnumMap;

enum Size {
  SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE
}

public class EnumMapExample {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    EnumMap<Size, String> sizeMap = new EnumMap<>(Size.class);
    sizeMap.put(Size.SMALL, "S");
    sizeMap.put(Size.MEDIUM, "M");
    sizeMap.put(Size.LARGE, "L");

    System.out.println("Size Map: " + sizeMap);
    System.out.println("Size Map Get Medium: " + sizeMap.get(Size.MEDIUM));
  }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"`EnumSet` provides methods like `of`, `allOf`, and `noneOf` for creating sets of enum constants. `EnumMap` is used to associate values with enum keys, providing efficient lookups."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Using Enums"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Use enums instead of integer constants or String literals:** Enums provide type safety and improve code readability."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Use uppercase for enum constants:** This is the standard convention and improves readability."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Consider adding fields and methods to enums:** This allows you to associate data and behavior with each enum constant, making your code more expressive."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Use `EnumSet` and `EnumMap` for efficient collection operations:** These classes are optimized for use with enums and provide better performance than general-purpose collections."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**When comparing enums, use `==` instead of `.equals()`:**  Since enums are singletons, `==` is sufficient and more efficient."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enums are a powerful feature in Java that can significantly improve the quality of your code. By using enums effectively, you can enhance type safety, readability, and maintainability. Understanding the various features of enums, such as fields, methods, interface implementation, and specialized collection classes, allows you to leverage their full potential in your Java applications."}]
